      "message": "That\u0027s an excellent thought. I actually did experiment with it briefly in this CL locally. What I discovered, first of all, was that nothing in our test corpus does this :) So already, that\u0027s a strike against it.\n\nAlso, I found that tracking multiple vardecls would require maintaining an array/hash-set of pointers rather than a single pointer, and scanning the contents of init-expressions to ensure that we don\u0027t reorder code inappropriately, e.g.:\n\n```\nint a;\nint b;\na \u003d b;\nb \u003d 5;\n```\n\nIt\u0027s a contrived example, but if we naively rewrote the code, we would generate a use-before-decl:\n\n```\nint a \u003d b;\nint b \u003d 5;\n```\n\nPreventing this requires looking inside the init-expressions.\nAt the end of the day, this is a goofy little optimization and doesn\u0027t really have a huge impact, so I think keeping it small and tightly scoped is better. (That\u0027s also why I deleted the sister feature in the followup CL--it still works, it\u0027s just not worth the code size/complexity anymore.)",
